Regression tests, run on the system itself.

The root file system holds the suite in /usr/local/test. Boot it and type

	/usr/local/test/run.sh		all tests
	/usr/local/test/run.sh sh awk	only the named tests

run.sh prints PASS or FAIL for each test, and finally a line
"run.sh: N passed, M failed".

A test is cases/NAME, a shell script; cases/NAME.exp, its expected
output, standard output and standard error together; and optionally
cases/NAME.sed, which normalises output that legitimately varies, such
as times, clock ticks, process ids, and kernel addresses. run.sh runs
the script with sh, from the cases directory, passes its output through
the .sed, and compares the result with the .exp. Once cmp has found a
difference, the rest of the output is read and thrown away, so that a
failing test has finished before the next one starts. A test passes
when cmp prints nothing; the exit status is not used, since sh gives a pipeline
the first nonzero status it reaps, which depends on timing. To see why a test
fails, run it by hand:

	cd /usr/local/test/cases; sh NAME

utils holds the programs the tests use, compiled from utils/*.c by the
Makefile (test.mk), the data files, and fs.img, a small file system made
from utils/fs.proto, on which the tests run the file system checkers.

Each test works in a directory of its own, /tmp/NAME, which it removes
at the end. A test which changes anything else (the date, accounting,
the crontab, the at spool, a file in /etc or /usr/adm) puts it back
before it ends, and kills every process it started, so that no test
depends on which tests ran before it. Its output must not depend on
timing, and no test waits a fixed time for another process. A test
which interrupts a command starts it through utils/pidf, which writes
its own process id to a file and then executes the command; the
process which sends the signal (kill -2) waits until the file exists.
A signal which arrives before the exec kills pidf as it would kill the
command; where the command catches the signal, the sender waits as well
for a sign that the command is running, such as its first output. A test which waits for some other event, such as cron having
run a job, polls for its result with sleep 1 in a loop.
